# Persistence Adapters

## Overview

An adapter decides where Jido keeps Agent records. Jido ships seven
adapters. Each one stores the same bytes under the same keys, so you can move
from the ETS adapter in tests to Postgres in production without changing an
Agent.

| Adapter | Storage | Survives | Extra dependencies |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `Jido.Persistence.ETS` | Memory | Process exits, not a BEAM stop | None |
| `Jido.Persistence.File` | Local directory | BEAM restart | None |
| `Jido.Persistence.Mnesia` | Mnesia table | What your table copies allow | None (OTP) |
| `Jido.Persistence.Ecto` | Postgres or SQLite | Database durability | `ecto_sql` and a driver |
| `Jido.Persistence.Redis` | Redis | Redis durability | A Redis client |
| `Jido.Persistence.S3` | S3 objects | Object storage | An S3 client |
| `Jido.Persistence.Bedrock` | Bedrock KV | Bedrock durability | `bedrock`, `bedrock_raft` |

This Livebook runs ETS, File and Mnesia with the same Agent: it starts the
Agent, runs one Turn, hibernates it, and thaws it. The other adapters appear
as configuration blocks. For what an adapter stores, see
[Storage Model](persistence-storage-model.md). For the write rules, see
[Durability Model](durability-model.md).

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

## The Counter

```elixir
defmodule AdapterGuide.Counter do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "counter"

  state do
    schema(Zoi.object(%{count: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)}))
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/adapters")

    route "counter.add", as: :add, defaults: %{amount: 1} do
      action %{amount: amount},
        schema: Zoi.object(%{amount: Zoi.integer()}),
        context: context do
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | count: context.agent_state.count + amount}}
      end
    end
  end
end

{:ok, _} = Jido.start(namespace: "adapter-guide")
```

The cell returns `{:ok, pid}` for the default Jido instance. Persistence keys
need a stable namespace, so the instance sets `namespace: "adapter-guide"`.
Without one, a save returns `{:error, :stable_namespace_required}`.

The next cell defines `round_trip`. It starts the Agent with an explicit
store, adds 5, hibernates it, waits for the process to stop, and thaws it
from the same store:

```elixir
round_trip = fn store, id ->
  {:ok, pid} = Jido.start_agent(AdapterGuide.Counter, id: id, persistence: store)
  :ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(pid)

  signal = AdapterGuide.Counter.add_signal!(%{amount: 5})
  {:ok, _agent, []} = Jido.AgentServer.call(pid, signal)

  ref = Process.monitor(pid)
  :ok = Jido.hibernate(pid)
  receive do: ({:DOWN, ^ref, :process, ^pid, _} -> :ok)

  {:ok, thawed} = Jido.thaw(AdapterGuide.Counter, id, persistence: store)
  :ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(thawed)
  snapshot = Jido.AgentServer.snapshot(thawed)
  :ok = Jido.stop_agent(thawed)

  {snapshot.agent.state, snapshot.state_version}
end
```

The start writes revision 0 and the Turn writes revision 1. A thawed Agent
keeps both its state and its revision.

## ETS

```elixir
ets = {Jido.Persistence.ETS, table: :adapter_guide}

{round_trip.(ets, "ets-1"), :ets.info(:adapter_guide_records, :size)}
```

The result is `{{%{count: 5}, 1}, 1}`: the thawed state, revision 1, and one
row. The adapter adds `_records` to the base name.

| Option | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `:table` | `:jido_persistence` | Base name. The ETS table is `<table>_records`. |

The table is a public named `set`. While the `:jido` application runs, a
supervised owner process inherits it, so it outlives the process that
created it. All data is gone when the BEAM stops. Use ETS for tests and
development.

## File

```elixir
dir = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "jido-adapter-guide")
File.rm_rf!(dir)
file = {Jido.Persistence.File, path: dir}

{round_trip.(file, "file-1"), Path.wildcard(Path.join(dir, "records/*.bin")) |> length()}
```

The result is `{{%{count: 5}, 1}, 1}`. The record is one file at
`<path>/records/<hash>.bin`, where the hash is the URL-safe Base64 SHA-256 of
the storage key.

| Option | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `:path` | required | Base directory. Created on first write. |

Each write goes to a temporary file, then an atomic rename. A per-key lock
serializes writes within one BEAM. The adapter doesn't call `fsync`, so a
power loss or OS crash can lose the latest write. One BEAM instance must own
the directory. Don't share it between nodes or through different symbolic
links.

## Mnesia

The application owns Mnesia. Start it and create a `set` table with the
attributes `[:key, :value]`:

```elixir
{:ok, _} = Application.ensure_all_started(:mnesia)

case :mnesia.create_table(:jido_records, attributes: [:key, :value], ram_copies: [node()]) do
  {:atomic, :ok} -> :ok
  {:aborted, {:already_exists, :jido_records}} -> :ok
end
```

The cell returns `:ok`. This table holds RAM copies on one node. It doesn't
need a disk schema.

```elixir
mnesia = {Jido.Persistence.Mnesia, table: :jido_records}

{round_trip.(mnesia, "mnesia-1"), :mnesia.table_info(:jido_records, :size)}
```

The result is `{{%{count: 5}, 1}, 1}`.

| Option | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `:table` | required | An existing `set` table with attributes `[:key, :value]`. |

`:table` is the only accepted option. Keep the default record name, which
equals the table name. The adapter doesn't start Mnesia or create tables. It
waits up to 5,000 ms for the table. A wrong table type, a `local_content`
table, or an extra option fails with `:invalid_mnesia_options`. A missing
table or a stopped Mnesia fails with `{:mnesia_unavailable, reason}`. Durability follows your table copies:
`ram_copies` are lost when the nodes stop, and `disc_copies` survive.

Each compare-and-swap is one transaction with a write lock on the key. A call
from inside your own Mnesia transaction returns
`{:error, {:rejected, :nested_transaction}}`.

## Ecto

`Jido.Persistence.Ecto` stores records in Postgres or SQLite3. Add the
optional dependency and a driver:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
{:ecto_sql, "~> 3.14"},
{:postgrex, "~> 0.19"}
```

Without `ecto_sql` at compile time, the adapter reports that it requires the
optional `:ecto_sql` dependency. Create the table with a migration. Pin
`:version` so a later Jido release can't change an old migration:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.Repo.Migrations.CreateJidoPersistenceRecords do
  use Ecto.Migration

  def up, do: Jido.Persistence.Ecto.Migration.up(version: 1)
  def down, do: Jido.Persistence.Ecto.Migration.down(version: 1)
end
```

The migration creates `jido_persistence_records` with three binary columns:
`key` (primary key), `value` and `write_token`. It accepts `:table` and
`:prefix`. Use the same values in the adapter's schema and repo options.

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
children = [
  MyApp.Repo,
  {Jido,
   name: MyApp.Jido,
   namespace: "my-app",
   persistence: {Jido.Persistence.Ecto, repo: MyApp.Repo}}
]
```

The instance store applies to Agents started with `persistence: :inherit`,
and to every `Jido.thaw/4`.

| Option | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `:repo` | required | A started `Ecto.Repo` with the Postgres or SQLite3 adapter. |
| `:schema` | `Jido.Persistence.Ecto.Record` | A schema with binary `key`, `value` and `write_token` fields. |
| `:repo_options` | `[]` | Passed to each repo call, such as `prefix:`. `:on_conflict` and `:conflict_target` are rejected. |

`write_token` changes on every successful write, so the affected-row count
stays correct even when the new bytes equal the old bytes.

## Redis

`Jido.Persistence.Redis` doesn't depend on a Redis client. You pass a
function that runs one command:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
children = [
  {Redix, name: MyApp.Redix},
  {Jido,
   name: MyApp.Jido,
   namespace: "my-app",
   persistence:
     {Jido.Persistence.Redis,
      command_fn: &Redix.command(MyApp.Redix, &1), prefix: "my-app"}}
]
```

| Option | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `:command_fn` | required | Arity-1 function. Takes a command list, returns `{:ok, result}` or `{:error, reason}`. |
| `:prefix` | `"jido"` | The Redis key is `"<prefix>:<key>"`. |
| `:ttl` | none | Positive key lifetime in milliseconds. |

A compare-and-swap is one Lua `EVAL`, so the connection must permit `EVAL`.
A `command_fn` error during a compare-and-swap is reported as indeterminate.
Durability follows your Redis persistence settings (AOF or RDB). A `:ttl`
expires active records and tombstones alike.

## S3

`Jido.Persistence.S3` stores one object per record and uses conditional
`PUT` requests. You supply the signed HTTP call:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
persistence =
  {Jido.Persistence.S3,
   bucket: "my-app-agents",
   prefix: "jido/",
   request_fn: &MyApp.S3Client.request/1}
```

`request_fn` receives a map with `:method` (`:get`, `:put` or `:delete`),
`:bucket`, `:key`, `:headers`, `:body`, `:timeout_ms`, `:retry` (`false`) and
`:follow_redirects` (`false`). It returns
`{:ok, %{status: integer, headers: headers, body: binary, code: s3_code_or_nil}}`
or `{:error, reason}`. Your client owns credentials, signing, pools and the
endpoint. It must not retry writes or follow redirects.

| Option | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `:bucket` | required | Bucket name. |
| `:request_fn` | required | Arity-1 function that signs and sends one request. |
| `:prefix` | `"jido/"` | Object key prefix. The object key is `prefix <> "k_" <> base64url(key)`. |
| `:timeout_ms` | `5_000` | Per-request limit, at most `120_000`. |

Unknown or duplicate options are rejected. Objects are limited to 5,000,000
bytes. Each write sends a SHA-256 checksum, and each read validates the
`x-amz-checksum-sha256` header before it returns an ETag-based token. Each
save is at least one HTTP round trip, so S3 suits low-frequency Agents.

## Bedrock

`Jido.Persistence.Bedrock` stores records in a Bedrock KV cluster. Add the
optional dependencies and supervise the cluster in your application:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
{:bedrock, "~> 0.7.2"},
{:bedrock_raft, "~> 0.10.0"}
```

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
persistence = {Jido.Persistence.Bedrock, repo: MyApp.BedrockRepo}
```

The instance store applies to Agents started with `persistence: :inherit`,
and to every `Jido.thaw/4`.

| Option | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `:repo` | required | A module defined with `use Bedrock.Repo`. |
| `:prefix` | `"jido:persistence:"` | Non-empty key prefix. |
| `:timeout_in_ms` | `5_000` | Positive integer or `:infinity`. |

Each compare-and-swap is one strictly serializable transaction with
`retry_limit: 0`, so a lost commit reply is reported as indeterminate, not
retried into a false conflict. Keys are limited to 16 KiB and values to
128 KiB. Use Bedrock's strict durability mode in production.

## Choose an Adapter

| You need | Use |
| --- | --- |
| Tests and local development | ETS |
| One node, data that survives a restart, no database | File |
| An existing Mnesia cluster | Mnesia |
| A relational database you already run | Ecto with Postgres |
| One local database file | Ecto with SQLite3 |
| Shared low-latency storage across nodes | Redis |
| Rarely written Agents in object storage | S3 |
| Strictly serializable distributed KV | Bedrock |

Every Turn on a persisted Agent writes one record before it commits. Choose
an adapter whose write latency fits your Turn rate.

To store records somewhere else, see
[Custom Adapters](custom-persistence-adapter.md).

## Clean Up

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop()
:stopped = :mnesia.stop()
File.rm_rf!(dir)
```

The cell stops the Jido instance and Mnesia and deletes the File adapter's
directory. It returns the list of deleted paths.

